      "latency_ms": 16638.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1154,
      "output": "Water is a chemical substance that is essential for all known forms of life. Chemically, it is defined by the formula **$H_2O$**, which means it is composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is and why it is unique:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. This means that the electrons in the water molecule are not shared equally between the oxygen and the hydrogen atoms. The oxygen atom has a slight negative charge, while the hydrogen atoms have a slight positive charge. This polarity allows water molecules to stick together (a property called **cohesion**) and to stick to other substances (adhesion).\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWhile we usually think of water as a liquid, it exists in all three states of matter under standard conditions:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When water is frozen, the molecules lock into a rigid, crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we encounter most often. The molecules are close together but can move past one another.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules gain enough energy to break free from the liquid and float into the air.\n\n### 3. Unique Properties\nWater has several physical properties that make it unlike other common liquids:\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it is the main transport medium in the human body and in the environment.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water has a strong \"skin\" on its surface, which allows insects to walk on it and for water to form droplets rather than spreading out flat.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This helps regulate the temperature of the Earth (oceans) and the human body.\n\n### 4. Importance\n*   **Biological Role:** Water makes up about 60% of the human body. It is used to transport nutrients, remove waste, and regulate body temperature.\n*   **Planetary Role:** Water covers about 71% of the Earth's surface and acts as a climate regulator, stabilizing temperatures to make the planet habitable."
